  "notes": [
    "Cross-check: every msg_hex in this file was decoded back to bytes and re-hashed with Python hashlib (CPython 3.12.14, OpenSSL-backed). All 9 sha384 and 9 sha512 digests reproduce exactly -- ALL_VERIFIED=true.",
    "The same digest strings were independently produced by `openssl dgst -sha384/-sha512` on the identical byte strings (see work/verify_sha2.py output recorded in manifest.md).",
    "Source per vector: 'empty' and 'abc' are the standard FIPS 180-4 / CAVP SHA*ShortMsg fixtures; 'fips180_2block_112B' is the worked SHA-512 example 2 from FIPS 180-2 (reprinted in FIPS 180-4's test-vector references). All 'a_repeat_*' and 'long_128B_00_7f' vectors are generated, not quoted: they are deterministic byte patterns (0x61 repeated n times, or 0x00..0x7F once each), so they are reproducible without shipping a source file.",
    "Why these lengths: SHA-512 processes 128-byte blocks and its padding is 0x80 || zeros || a 16-byte big-endian bit length, so a message of L bytes fits in one block iff L + 1 + 16 <= 128, i.e. L <= 111. Therefore 111 -> 1 block, 112 -> 2 blocks (the first length that spills over), 127 -> 2 blocks, 128 -> 3 blocks (exact block multiple still forces a full extra padding block), 129 -> 3 blocks. This is the SHA-512 analogue of SHA-256's 55/56 boundary.",
    "Digest widths: sha384 digests are 48 bytes (96 hex chars), sha512 digests are 64 bytes (128 hex chars); both were asserted for every record. msg_hex is always lowercase hex with no prefix or separator.",
    "msg_len and msg_desc are auxiliary fields added for readability; the load-bearing fields are name, msg_hex and digest_hex."
  ]
}
